{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "sqlpackage-import-export",
  "domain": "data-movement",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "multi-step-with-silent-failures"],
  "correction": "Agents treat the dacpac and bacpac extensions as what decides what moves, when what actually decides it is which SqlPackage action ran and which property was set: Extract and Publish carry schema only by default, Export and Import always carry schema plus every base table's row data, and Extract can be told, with ExtractAllTableData, to fold row data into a file still named and shaped like a schema-only dacpac. Agents also read a completed publish or import as proof the database is now backed up, when Azure SQL Database has no BACKUP or RESTORE T-SQL and neither bacpac nor dacpac is a supported recovery mechanism. Agents assume Import behaves like Publish, when Import refuses outright, with error SQL71659, against any target database that already holds a single user object, while Publish diffs an existing target and reruns safely; both actions create the target database when it does not exist and the connected principal has permission to create one, so the difference between them is the refusal, not the creation. Finally, agents export a live database without scanning it first, when a database still carrying Windows-authenticated users, server-login-mapped users or leftover Service Broker permissions fails export with SQL71627 only after schema extraction has already run, discarding the whole attempt rather than the offending object.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer chooses the action by what actually has to move and by whether the target already holds objects, checks the source for principals the target cannot carry before the long running step, and separates this copy operation from the question of what the database's real backup is.",
    "task": "We need a copy of our production Azure SQL Database in a brand new database on our development server, schema and data both, and we want to repeat it every Monday morning. I produced a file from production last week and now I want to lay it down on the development side.\n\nThree things I am unsure about. The development database does not exist yet, so do I have to go and create an empty one first? On the second Monday that database will already have last week's tables in it, so will the same command just put the new copy over the top? And our runbook currently names this weekly file as the backup of record for the application database, which I would like to confirm before the audit next month.\n\nAlso, last week's run against production failed part of the way through, complaining about a user, after it had already been going for twenty minutes. Tell me the sequence to use.",
    "states": [
      "What decides whether row data travels is which action was run and which properties were set, not the file's name or extension.",
      "The action that always carries data refuses a target that already holds any user object, so the second Monday needs a fresh target or the action that diffs an existing one.",
      "The target database is created automatically when it is absent and the connected principal is allowed to create one, so creating an empty database first is not required.",
      "Neither of these file types is a supported recovery mechanism for this service, so the runbook should name the service's own automatic backups instead.",
      "A principal the target cannot honour fails the export only after schema extraction has already run, discarding the whole attempt, so the source should be scanned for such principals first."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The target database has to exist before the operation runs, because the tool will not create a database that is absent.",
      "The file extension is what determines whether row data is included in the file.",
      "A schema and data file of this kind is a supported backup and recovery mechanism for an Azure SQL Database.",
      "The data carrying import can be re-run against a target that already contains user objects and will overwrite or merge them.",
      "The failure naming a user is a transient fault or a permissions problem on the tool's own connection, so retrying will get past it."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "execute"],
  // REPRIEVED 2026-09-20, and this comment replaces one that is now wrong. The
  // reading of 2026-09-05 returned NEITHER and this field was lowered on it, but
  // that reading rested on three runs per arm. Re-measured at ten, the verdict is
  // LIFT: the statement that a principal the target cannot honour fails the export
  // only after schema extraction has already run reads 9 of 10 with the skill
  // against 6 of 10 without. A thirty point gap with ten runs behind it is exactly
  // what the rule asks for, so the skill does change the answer and the earlier
  // reading was the sample size talking.
  //
  // It stays at draft only because raising a maturity is a judgement nobody has
  // made yet, not because the evidence is missing. The evidence now supports
  // preview and the promotion backlog in check-maturity-drift.mjs says so.
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "sql-database-projects",
    "restore-and-recover",
    "bulk-load-and-bulk-copy",
    "provision-azure-sql-db"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a source database, container or cloud, holding at least one table with rows and one view",
      "SqlPackage installed as a global .NET tool",
      "a second, empty target database to import or publish into",
      "a third, non-empty target database to demonstrate the import refusal"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "sqlpackage /Action:Extract against the source database produces a file whose only entries are model.xml, DacMetadata.xml, Origin.xml and Content_Types.xml, with no Data folder",
      "sqlpackage /Action:Export against the same source produces a file that additionally contains a Data/<schema>.<table>/TableData-*.BCP entry for every base table holding rows, and no such entry for a view",
      "sqlpackage /Action:Extract with /p:ExtractAllTableData=true produces a .dacpac file that also contains a Data/<schema>.<table>/TableData-*.BCP entry",
      "sqlpackage /Action:Import into the non-empty target fails with error SQL71659 and the target is refused before any change is made",
      "sqlpackage /Action:Import into the empty target succeeds and the row counts in the target match the source",
      "sqlpackage /Action:Publish of the extracted dacpac against an already-published target completes with no pending changes and exit code 0",
      "BACKUP DATABASE issued as T-SQL against the source database is rejected with error 40510"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /version:True",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "Every command in this skill is a SqlPackage command, so the tool being installed and able to print its build number with no database in reach is the precondition for all of them. Measured 2026-09-02, exit 0, build 170.4.83.3."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /Action:Extract /help:True | grep -q '^/p:ExtractAllTableData=(BOOLEAN .False.)$'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The property this skill turns on, checked to exist for the Extract action and to default to False, out of the build's own help. Measured 2026-09-02 on 170.4.83.3. If a future build renames it or changes the default, the claim that a dacpac is schema only until this is set stops being true, and this probe goes red first."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /Action:Export /help:True | grep -q '^/p:IgnoreUserLoginMappings=' && exit 1 || exit 0",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The asymmetry the skill routes on: Extract can be told to drop user-to-login mappings and Export cannot, so a database that fails export can still be extracted. Measured 2026-09-02 on 170.4.83.3, the property is absent from the Export help. This probe is deliberately the negative, because the skill's advice becomes wrong the day Export gains it."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /Action:Extract /help:True | grep -q '^/p:IgnoreUserLoginMappings=(BOOLEAN .False.)$'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The positive half of the same pair, so a red run on the previous probe can be told apart from SqlPackage renaming or dropping the property everywhere. Measured 2026-09-02 on 170.4.83.3."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /Action:Export /TargetFile:/tmp/probe-sqlpackage.bacpac /SourceServerName:127.0.0.1,$PROBE_PORT /SourceDatabaseName:$PROBE_DB /p:ExportAllTableData=true 2>&1 | grep -q \"is not a valid argument for the 'Export' action\"",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "An invented property is refused by name before any connection is opened, which is what makes a property claim checkable for free. Measured 2026-09-02 on 170.4.83.3: exit 1 and that message, with no connection line in the output, against a port nothing is listening on. This is the probe shape that gives the catalog a cheap way to fail a made-up flag."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition;",
        "expect": { "rows": [[5]] },
        "why": "The precondition for the no-BACKUP claim. Msg 40510 for BACKUP DATABASE is a property of EngineEdition 5, so the probe below run against anything else would be measuring a different product. Measured 2026-08-29 against 12.0.2000.8 reporting Edition SQL Azure."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @db sysname = DB_NAME();\nBACKUP DATABASE @db TO DISK = '/tmp/probe-sqlpackage.bak';",
        "expect": { "error": 40510 },
        "why": "The reason a bacpac gets reached for as a backup, made executable: there is no BACKUP statement on this service, so a bacpac is the only thing that looks like one and it is not one. Measured 2026-08-29 on the container as BACKUP DATABASE against a literal existing database name, Msg 40510. The variable form is used here only so the probe can name the scratch database it is handed; BACKUP DATABASE { database_name | @database_name_var } is the documented syntax (https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/backup-transact-sql). If this ever succeeds, this skill's routing to restore-and-recover needs rewriting."
      }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "DECLARE @sql nvarchar(max) = N'BACKUP DATABASE ' + QUOTENAME(DB_NAME()) + N' TO DISK = ''/tmp/probe_sqlpkg.bak'';';\nEXEC sp_executesql @sql;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 40510
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. BACKUP DATABASE is not available on this engine, which is why a data tier package is the export path rather than a backup file. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sp_99 (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sp_99 (v) SELECT CAST(JSON_ARRAY(CAST(value AS float), 1.0, 2.0) AS vector(3)) FROM GENERATE_SERIES(1, 99);\nGO\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX probe_sp_99_ix ON dbo.probe_sp_99 (v) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 42266
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The 100 row minimum is why a bacpac import fails: the import creates schema objects before it loads data, so the index is built against an empty table. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sp_tr (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sp_tr (v) SELECT CAST(JSON_ARRAY(CAST(value AS float), 1.0, 2.0) AS vector(3)) FROM GENERATE_SERIES(1, 150);\nGO\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX probe_sp_tr_ix ON dbo.probe_sp_tr (v) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');\nGO\nTRUNCATE TABLE dbo.probe_sp_tr;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 42232
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. And why reloading in place hits the same wall one step earlier: the truncate that would empty the table is refused while the index exists. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Export my Azure SQL database to a bacpac file",
      "How do I copy this database to another server with sqlpackage",
      "What is the difference between a dacpac and a bacpac",
      "Extract a dacpac from my Azure SQL database"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "The bacpac export failed with SQL71627 partway through, what happened",
      "sqlpackage import says the database is not empty and it stopped",
      "I want to move this database from the container to a real Azure SQL server",
      "Someone asked me to back up the database by exporting a bacpac before a migration"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Set up point-in-time restore for my Azure SQL database",
      "Bulk load this CSV file into my orders table",
      "Build a SQL database project and add a post-deployment script",
      "Provision a new Azure SQL Database server and open the firewall"
    ]
  }
}
